@*
    The part of a file pane that stays put: which file this is, and the three
    things a reader does to it.

    A component of its own because it is the half of FileView that does not move.
    The body scrolls, swaps between a rendering, a comparison and a host's editor,
    and is re-parsed on the way; none of that changes the line that says which
    file the reader is looking at. Two components make that separation something
    the compiler holds rather than something a comment claims — and they let a
    surface that is not FileView draw the same header, which is what the storybook
    does on its own page for this.

    Nothing here reads the file. The record arrives parsed, the detail line arrives
    as the three facts it is made of, and the mode flags arrive already decided.
    That is deliberate: the header's buttons and the body's content have to agree
    about which mode the pane is in, and two components each deciding for
    themselves is exactly how they come to disagree. FileView decides once and
    tells both — see OffersEdit and OffersCompare, which are the answer to "would
    pressing this do anything?" rather than to "may this file be edited?".

    Which leaves this free to be used on its own. Every parameter is a value or a
    callback, there is no ambient state and no cascading value, and everything
    optional draws nothing when it is absent: no record, no path, no actions, no
    baselines. A header with nothing but a name renders the name.

    The layout is two lines, and everything in them is here for reasons recorded
    where it is drawn — the record around the name, the path right-aligned on the
    name's line just before the status the record closes it with, then a second
    line shared by the detail line on the left and, right-aligned opposite it, the
    action group named for the file with the baseline picker beside it while a
    comparison is running against more than one version.

    Two lines and not two columns because the first one grows: a path is a machine
    string as long as the tree it sits in, and buttons opposite it were taking the
    room it needed and crowding the status at the same time. What the file is holds
    the width; what you may do to it sits under. The stylesheet carries the rest of
    that argument, on .file-view__header.

    Two and not four, which is what it had quietly become. The count was written
    for an identity column of one line, and the details — source, kind, size — are
    drawn under the name inside that column, so any file stating one of the three
    had a third line and pushed the acts to a fourth; comparing against several
    baselines bought a fifth. The details are on the second line now rather than in
    the column, sharing it with the acts, and the picker is beside the acts rather
    than under them. Nothing else earns a line: the name and the details truncate
    where the path already did, and neither group of controls wraps.

    ---

    The markup below is indented as though it were still inside FileView's
    `<article>`, and it has to stay that way. A newline between two sibling
    elements is a text node in the rendered output, indentation included — Razor
    only trims the whitespace at an element's edges and around a code block — so
    re-indenting this file would change the bytes every host ships between the
    identity column and the aside. There is no visual difference either way; there
    is a diff, and a diff in shipped markup should mean somebody changed the
    markup.
*@

    <header class="file-view__header">
        <div class="@IdentityClass">
            @* The file's own status, beside the file's own name.

               A knowledge file states it in a fence under its title, and read in
               the body that fence is at the top of a document — the one place a
               reader has already scrolled past by the time they think to ask
               whether any of it is still current. The header is the part of this
               pane that stays put, and it is where the name it describes already
               is, so the record is drawn around the name rather than beside it:
               one line, the name and the state of it.

               The chapters keep theirs. A `##` fence describes the chapter under
               it, that heading is in the body, and it stays there — drawn by
               MetadataChapterView, which is the other shape a record takes.

               MetadataFileView owns the fallback this branch used to make here:
               a file that says nothing about itself still has to be named, and a
               record with nothing in it draws no element at all.

               The same fragment goes into both parameters, and that is not an
               oversight. The two used to be different markup — an item of the
               record's headline row, or this column's own content — and the path
               joining the name's line collapsed the difference: whatever holds
               the name now has to be a row, so with no record the column is the
               row and the items are the same two either way. The parameters stay
               two because MetadataFileView is not only this header's, and a
               caller whose heading is not a row still needs the other one. *@
            <MetadataFileView Metadata="@Metadata"
                              Vocabulary="@Vocabulary"
                              ShowFields="false"
                              OnStatusChanged="@OnStatusChanged"
                              OnNavigate="@OnNavigate"
                              HrefFor="@HrefFor"
                              AriaLabel="@($"{Name} metadata")"
                              CssClass="file-view__record"
                              TestId="@Suffix("file-metadata")"
                              Heading="@NameLine"
                              HeadingAlone="@NameLine" />
        </div>

        @* Drawn only when it has something in it. As a column beside the identity
           an empty second line was a zero-width neighbour and cost nothing; as a
           line under it, an empty one still takes the header's gap, and a file pane
           that knows nothing about the file and offers no acts would carry a strip
           of space saying so.

           The two share it because neither is what the file is — that is the line
           above — and because they are what the eye reaches after the name. Which
           is also why the details are here rather than in the identity column,
           where they used to be drawn under the name and where they were the third
           line of a header that claims two. *@
        @if (Details.Count > 0 || HasAside)
        {
        <div class="file-view__summary">
            @if (Details.Count > 0)
            {
                <p class="file-view__meta">@string.Join(" · ", Details)</p>
            }

            @if (HasAside)
            {
            <div class="file-view__aside">
            @if (AllowCopy || OffersEdit || OffersCompare)
            {
                @* Named as a group, because "Edit" and "Compare" on their own say
                   nothing about which of several files on a page they belong to
                   (.design/accessibility.md). The file's name is what
                   distinguishes them, and it is already here. *@
                <div class="file-view__actions" role="group" aria-label="@($"{Name} actions")">
                    @if (AllowCopy)
                    {
                        @* The file, not the rendering: the source is what round
                           trips, which is the same call MarkdownView makes. *@
                        <CopyButton Text="@(Body ?? string.Empty)"
                                    Label="@($"{CopyLabel} {Name}")"
                                    ButtonCssClass="file-view__action file-view__action--copy"
                                    StatusCssClass="file-view__copy-status"
                                    ButtonTestId="@Suffix("copy")"
                                    StatusTestId="@Suffix("copy-status")" />
                    }

                    @if (OffersEdit)
                    {
                        @* A mark rather than a word, because the row it is in is
                           the header of a file and not a toolbar: the file's name
                           is what a reader is here to read. The word survives
                           whole on the label and the tooltip, which is where an
                           icon-only control has to keep it
                           (.design/accessibility.md). *@
                        <IconButton CssClass="file-view__action"
                                    AriaLabel="@EditActionLabel"
                                    Title="@EditActionLabel"
                                    OnClick="() => OnToggleEditing.InvokeAsync()"
                                    TestId="@Suffix(Editing ? "done" : "edit")">@(Editing ? DoneIcon : EditIcon)</IconButton>
                    }

                    @if (OffersCompare)
                    {
                        @* A toggle and not a button whose word flips: comparing is
                           a state the pane is in, and aria-pressed is how that is
                           said without renaming the control underneath a reader.
                           Still a toggle now that the word is a glyph — the state
                           is the reason for the control, and an icon that changed
                           nothing a screen reader could hear would only be the
                           picture of one. *@
                        @* Dressed as the two marks beside it and not as a toggle.
                           A toggle's own base is the full button — the wider
                           padding, and a tinted, bordered box once it is pressed —
                           so comparing drew a raised control between two plain
                           glyphs, and the row read as one act that mattered more
                           than the others rather than as three things you may do
                           to a file. It takes IconButton's base instead, which is
                           what Copy and Edit are, and says it is on by the colour
                           of the mark.

                           Still a toggle underneath: aria-pressed is set the same
                           either way, so what changes here is the paint and not
                           what a screen reader is told. *@
                        <ToggleButton Pressed="Comparing"
                                      PressedChanged="@OnComparingChanged"
                                      BaseClass="btn btn--icon"
                                      PressedCssClass="file-view__action--on"
                                      CssClass="file-view__action"
                                      AriaLabel="@CompareLabel"
                                      Title="@CompareLabel"
                                      TestId="@Suffix("compare")"><span aria-hidden="true">@CompareIcon</span></ToggleButton>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            @if (Comparing && CompareBaselines.Count > 1)
            {
                @* Only while comparing, and only when there is a choice. A picker
                   for one baseline is a label that looks like a control, and a
                   picker beside a file nobody is comparing is a question nobody
                   asked. *@
                <div class="file-view__baselines" role="group" aria-label="@($"Compare {Name} against")">
                    @foreach (var baseline in CompareBaselines)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="file-view__baseline"
                                      PressedCssClass="file-view__baseline--current"
                                      Pressed="@IsSelected(baseline)"
                                      PressedChanged="() => OnSelectBaseline.InvokeAsync(baseline)"
                                      TestId="@Suffix($"baseline-{baseline.Id}")">@baseline.Label</ToggleButton>
                    }
                </div>
            }
            </div>
            }
        </div>
        }
    </header>

    @* Everything else the record states, in a strip of its own under the header.

       Under it and not in it, because the header is two lines and a record
       stating `related` and `depends-on` is a row per field: the fields were the
       one thing left that could still push it past the count, and no amount of
       truncating inside the header would have fixed it — the rows are the
       content, not an overflow of it.

       A strip is where this file already puts a band of facts about the file that
       is not the file: see .file-view__frontmatter, which sits in the same place
       for the same reason. Drawn only when the record states something other than
       its status, so a file with nothing but a status draws the header and the
       body with nothing between them. *@
    @if (ShowMetadataFields && Metadata is { IsEmpty: false } record && MetadataFields.HasAny(record))
    {
    <div class="file-view__record-fields">
        <MetadataFields Metadata="@record"
                        OnNavigate="@OnNavigate"
                        HrefFor="@HrefFor"
                        TestId="@Suffix("file-metadata-fields")" />
    </div>
    }
@code {
    /// <summary>The header's left-hand column, as it reads: the file's name, and
    /// nothing else.
    ///
    /// <para>One fragment, drawn whether or not there is a record to put it in, so
    /// the two cannot say the file's name differently.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>The details used to be here too, under the name. They are on the second
    /// line now — a header that claims two lines cannot draw a third under its own
    /// heading — and what is left is a wrapper around a single element. It stays a
    /// wrapper: it is the item the name's row aligns, and the row's other two items
    /// are positioned against it by name in the stylesheet and asserted by name in
    /// <c>FileViewHeaderLayoutTests</c>.</para></summary>
    private RenderFragment IdentityLines => __builder =>
    {
        @* The mark and the name written as two code blocks with nothing between
           them. Razor trims the whitespace either side of a code block, so a header
           given no mark renders exactly the bytes it always did, and one given a
           mark gains no space before the file's name — the gap is the stylesheet's,
           in em, the same as the one in front of a chapter heading. *@
        <h3 class="file-view__name">@NameMark@Name</h3>
    };

    /// <summary>The name's line: the name, the path right-aligned in whatever is
    /// left of it, and — when there is a record — the status closing it.
    ///
    /// <para>The row itself belongs to whatever is holding this. With a record that
    /// is <c>.knowledge-record__headline</c>, which appends the status after these
    /// two items; without one it is the identity column, which has nothing else in
    /// it. So the items are the same either way and one fragment serves both,
    /// which is why <see cref="MetadataFileView.Heading"/> and
    /// <see cref="MetadataFileView.HeadingAlone"/> are given the same value.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>The name is wrapped rather than handed over loose. It was wrapped
    /// because the details were under it and loose they would have been a second
    /// item of the row; with the details gone to the line below, the wrapper is what
    /// the row's other two items are aligned against — the path grows into what it
    /// leaves and the status closes the line after it, both by name in the
    /// stylesheet. Unwrapping it would make the <c>h3</c> the item those rules mean,
    /// which is a rename of the row's parts for no gain.</para></summary>
    private RenderFragment NameLine => __builder =>
    {
        <div class="file-view__headline">@IdentityLines</div>

        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Path))
        {
            @* After the name and before the status, which is the order a reader
               asks for them in: which file, where it is, and whether it is still
               current. The status is what fixes it — the record draws the status
               at the right edge of the row the name is on, so anything that is
               to precede it has to be an item of that row too. In the aside,
               which is where this used to be, the path could only ever follow.

               The row's one growing item, so it takes the space between the name
               and the status and its right alignment lands it against the status
               rather than against the name. Truncated visually when the header is
               narrow, never in the DOM, so a screen reader still reads the whole
               path. *@
            <p class="file-view__path">@Path</p>
        }
    };

    /// <summary>The file's name — the title, and what every control here is named
    /// for.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// A mark drawn inside the name, in front of it. Nothing by default, and
    /// nothing is what almost every file pane in this product passes.
    ///
    /// <para>Inside the <c>h3</c> rather than beside it, because what it describes
    /// is the file and the <c>h3</c> is where this header says which file that is.
    /// Beside it, it would be a second item of the name's row — competing with the
    /// path and the status for a line that is already three things wide.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>A fragment and not a value, which keeps the promise the top of this
    /// file makes: nothing here reads the file, and nothing here decides what a
    /// mark means. Whether a <c>.domain</c> file's <c>type</c> has a glyph is a
    /// question about a folder and a vocabulary, and <c>FileView</c> is the
    /// component that was told both.</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? NameMark { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The full path, right-aligned on the name's own line and just
    /// before the status. Absent draws no element.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Path { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Who or what the file comes from, e.g. "GitHub Copilot". The first
    /// of the three facts the detail line is made of.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Source { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What kind of file it is, e.g. "Path-specific instructions".
    ///
    /// <para>Whatever the host means by kind, printed as given. FileView resolves
    /// a code file's language into this when its caller named no kind, because
    /// choosing what a file is read as is the pane's decision and not the
    /// header's.</para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Kind { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Size on disk. Null when the caller does not know it — better an
    /// absent fact than a fabricated zero.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public long? SizeInBytes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The file's own metadata record — the block under the title that
    /// opens it — or nothing.
    ///
    /// <para>Already parsed. Reading it is the pane's job: the text the record
    /// comes from is the text the body is showing, and a header that read it a
    /// second time could describe a different file than the one on screen. A
    /// record that states nothing draws as none: see
    /// <see cref="MetadataFileView"/>, which owns that fallback.</para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MetadataRecord? Metadata { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The status words the file's own record may state, and how each is
    /// drawn. Without one the status is still shown, just not judged.
    ///
    /// <para>A vocabulary and not a <c>KnowledgeFolder</c>, which is what this took
    /// before. The folder had exactly one reader here — resolving it to a
    /// vocabulary on the way to <see cref="MetadataFileView"/> — so it was a
    /// knowledge concept in a Layout component's public surface for one field's
    /// sake, which is the coupling the record views had just been relieved of.
    /// FileView keeps the folder, because a knowledge pane genuinely has one, and
    /// resolves it here.</para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MetadataStatusVocabulary Vocabulary { get; set; } = MetadataStatusVocabulary.None;

    /// <summary>Whether the rest of the record — everything it states but its
    /// status — is drawn at all. On by default; a surface that wants the status
    /// alone turns it off.
    ///
    /// <para>Not "in the header": the fields are never in it. They are a row per
    /// stated field, which is the one thing that could still push a two-line header
    /// to four, so they are drawn in a strip beneath it the way this pane already
    /// treats a file's frontmatter. What this parameter decides is whether that
    /// strip exists.</para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowMetadataFields { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>Raised with the status a reader picked for the file. Nothing here
    /// writes it, and nothing here knows which block it came from — the pane does,
    /// and reports it as the change it is.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string?> OnStatusChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with the reference a reader activated in the record.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KnowledgeReference> OnNavigate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Resolves a metadata reference to a link target; null leaves that
    /// reference as text rather than a broken link.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<KnowledgeReference, string?>? HrefFor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the file's own copy button is offered. Copies
    /// <see cref="Body"/>, so a header handed no text has nothing to copy and
    /// should leave this off.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool AllowCopy { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the copy button is called to a screen reader, before the
    /// file's name: "Copy file context-map.md".</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string CopyLabel { get; set; } = "Copy file";

    /// <summary>The file's contents, verbatim — what the copy button copies. Read
    /// for nothing else: the header never renders the file.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Body { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a way into editing is offered at all.
    ///
    /// <para>"Would pressing this do anything?", and not "may this file be
    /// edited?". Permission is the pane's question and it takes more than
    /// permission to answer — there has to be a writable body to switch to, or a
    /// host listening for the switch. A button that announced an edit and left the
    /// same file on screen would be a lie, and the pane is the only thing that
    /// knows.</para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool OffersEdit { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Which mode the body is in, so the one button that changes meaning
    /// can say which of the two it is right now.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Editing { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when the edit control was pressed. Not a two-way binding:
    /// the modes are exclusive and the pane enforces that, so this asks rather
    /// than tells.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnToggleEditing { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string EditLabel { get; set; } = "Edit";

    [Parameter]
    public string DoneLabel { get; set; } = "Done";

    /// <summary>The mark the way into editing wears. A pencil, which is what this
    /// product already draws an edit with everywhere else it draws one — a
    /// vocabulary is only a vocabulary if it is the same word each time.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string EditIcon { get; set; } = "✎";

    /// <summary>And the mark for leaving. A tick, for the same reason.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string DoneIcon { get; set; } = "✓";

    /// <summary>Whether a comparison is worth offering — which is the pane's
    /// question for the reason <see cref="OffersEdit"/> is, and a narrower one: a
    /// file whose baselines all match it is a press that shows the reader what
    /// they are already looking at.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool OffersCompare { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the comparison is showing, which is what the toggle
    /// reports as its pressed state.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Comparing { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when the compare toggle was pressed, with the state asked
    /// for. Not a two-way binding, for the reason
    /// <see cref="OnToggleEditing"/> is not.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<bool> OnComparingChanged { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string CompareLabel { get; set; } = "Compare";

    /// <summary>The mark comparing wears. Two arrows facing each other, which is
    /// as close as this product's glyph vocabulary gets to a diff — nothing here
    /// already meant "these two texts, side by side". A parameter so a host with
    /// a better one is not stuck with this.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string CompareIcon { get; set; } = "⇄";

    /// <summary>The versions worth comparing the file against, in the order a
    /// reader would reach for them. The picker is drawn only while
    /// <see cref="Comparing"/> and only when there is more than one.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<FileCompareBaseline> CompareBaselines { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Which baseline is being compared against, already resolved.
    ///
    /// <para>The id and not the index, and resolved rather than named: "the one
    /// asked for, or the first offered" is a rule about a list the pane owns and
    /// may have just changed, so the pane applies it and the picker highlights
    /// what it is told.</para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? SelectedBaselineId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with the baseline a reader picked.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<FileCompareBaseline> OnSelectBaseline { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The pane's test id, which every control here derives its own
    /// from. Null leaves them all off.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The identity column, and whether it is holding a record.
    ///
    /// <para>The column takes the header's free space either way — that is what
    /// puts the far end of the name's line at the far end of the header, which is
    /// where the path and the status are both aligned to. What the modifier says is
    /// which of the two shapes the column is in: holding a record, whose headline
    /// is the row, or holding the name's line directly, in which case the column
    /// <em>is</em> the row and the stylesheet makes it one.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>A record that states nothing is not one, which is the same question
    /// MetadataFileView asks of what it is handed — asking it the same way here
    /// keeps the column and its content from disagreeing about whether there is a
    /// record.</para></summary>
    private string IdentityClass =>
        Metadata is { IsEmpty: false }
            ? "file-view__identity file-view__identity--record"
            : "file-view__identity";

    /// <summary>Whether the second line's trailing group has anything in it: an
    /// act, or a baseline picker that is showing. The same two conditions the
    /// group's own children ask, asked once above them so the group itself can be
    /// absent rather than empty — an empty one would still take the line's gap and
    /// push the details off the right edge they are measured against.</summary>
    private bool HasAside =>
        AllowCopy || OffersEdit || OffersCompare || (Comparing && CompareBaselines.Count > 1);

    /// <summary>What the one button that changes meaning is called right now. Both
    /// the accessible name and the tooltip read it, because an icon-only control
    /// has nowhere else to say which of the two it is.</summary>
    private string EditActionLabel => Editing ? DoneLabel : EditLabel;

    private bool IsSelected(FileCompareBaseline baseline) =>
        SelectedBaselineId is not null
        && string.Equals(baseline.Id, SelectedBaselineId, StringComparison.Ordinal);

    private string? Suffix(string part) => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-{part}";

    /// <summary>The left of the header's second line: everything known about the
    /// file that is not its name or its path, in the order a reader asks for
    /// it.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<string> Details =>
    [
        .. new[] { Source, Kind, SizeInBytes is { } bytes ? FormatSize(bytes) : null }
            .Where(part => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(part))
            .Select(part => part!)
    ];

    /// <summary>Bytes as the reader would say them. Formatted in the current
    /// culture, so the decimal separator is the one the rest of the app uses.
    ///
    /// <para>Static and internal because it is not only this header's: FolderView
    /// says a file's size the same way on the row that names it, and two
    /// formatters would eventually disagree about where the kilobyte
    /// starts.</para></summary>
    internal static string FormatSize(long bytes)
    {
        const long Kb = 1024;
        const long Mb = Kb * 1024;
        const long Gb = Mb * 1024;

        if (bytes < 0) return string.Empty;

        return bytes switch
        {
            < Kb => $"{bytes} B",
            < Mb => $"{bytes / (double)Kb:0.#} KB",
            < Gb => $"{bytes / (double)Mb:0.#} MB",
            _ => $"{bytes / (double)Gb:0.#} GB"
        };
    }
}
